Drop the GTK 3 popup shadow when the shadow setting is off

The GTK 3 sheet gives one shadow to the decoration node. GTK 3 draws that
node under a window that paints its own frame. It draws the node under a
menu, a combo box list and a tooltip as well, so one rule shadows them all.
The setting must reach the popups alone.

The emitter therefore appends one rule when the setting is off. The rule
takes the selectors .popup decoration and tooltip decoration, and it writes
box-shadow: none. Measurement on GTK 3.24.52 gives those two selectors. A
menu and a combo box list carry the class popup with a compositor and with
none. A tooltip carries the object name tooltip and no class popup. The
class csd reaches a popup with a compositor alone.

Neither selector reaches a toplevel window, so a window keeps its shadow.
The emitter appends the rule after the decoration rule, so the sheet of the
default setting keeps every byte that it holds today. The GTK 4 sheet holds
no decoration node and no popup shadow, so the two settings give one GTK 4
sheet. The fallback sheets come from the default settings and do not move.

The build compiles the Qt 6 parser into the emitter, so the emitter reads a
stylerc key through that parser alone. That parser therefore takes the
member, the read, the default and the write of popupShadow. The default is
true, which keeps the shadow that a user has today.

assert-shadow.sh reads the two arms of one build. It asserts that the on arm
holds the shadow of the decoration rule and no popup rule. It asserts that
the off arm holds the same shadow and one popup rule of box-shadow: none. It
asserts that the two GTK 4 arms match byte for byte. The script then breaks
each contract in a copy of a sheet. It fails if the check passes the copy.

Let the GTK sheet writer own its node names

The writer of the GTK sheets holds the node names in a reference member. Each
of the two build functions passes the return of nodesFor, which is a
temporary. That temporary ends at the constructor call, so the member points
at a stack frame that the program has left.

The emitter reads the node names through that member for the whole run. A
build with the address sanitizer therefore stops the writer at the first read,
in the button rules. A build without the sanitizer reads the same frame before
another call writes over it, so the sheets keep their content by chance alone.

The member takes value semantics. The writer copies the node names of the
dialect at construction, and it holds them for the whole run. The names are
nine strings and four flags, and every one of the strings is a literal, so the
copy allocates nothing.

The four shipped sheets keep every byte. A sanitizer build of the writer now
runs each of the four sheets to the end.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
fourth commit and the commit after it. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that
branch. Merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

